# WFORMTG.KEY - the editing rules of the secret field PASS: Backspace,
# Delete, overtyping a selection, Alt+Backspace, Ctrl+Backspace and
# paste, then the memo's clipboard; TEST/GOLDEN/WIN/WFORMTG.SNP.
# The snapshot shows one * for each character held.
K F5 STAB STAB STAB STAB
S FOCUS
T abcde
S TYPED
K BS
S BS
K HOME DEL
S DEL
K END DEL
S DELEND
K SHIFT-HOME
T x
S OVER
K ALT-BS
S ALTBS
K INS
S INS
# CTRL-BS types nothing: over a selection, which the next character
# then replaces, and at the caret.
K SHIFT-HOME CTRL-BS
T x
S CTRLBSEL
K CTRL-BS
S CTRLBS
# Put ZZ on the clipboard from TEXT, and show that it pastes into CODE.
K TAB TAB TAB TAB
T zz
K SHIFT-HOME CTRL-INS TAB SHIFT-INS
S CLIP
# Paste into PASS does nothing, by SHIFT-INS or CTRL-V.
K STAB STAB STAB STAB STAB
S BACKPASS
K SHIFT-INS
S PASTE
K CTRL-V
S CTRLV
# Cut deletes the selection without the clipboard; pasting still does nothing.
K HOME SHIFT-END SHIFT-DEL
S CUT
K SHIFT-INS
S PASTE2
# The clipboard still holds ZZ: the cut did not take what it deleted.
K STAB STAB STAB STAB STAB STAB STAB STAB STAB
K HOME SHIFT-END DEL
S CODEGONE
K SHIFT-INS
S CLIP2
# The memo, an OEM text control on the Notes page, puts its first line
# on the clipboard, and a copy with nothing selected leaves it there: it
# pastes at the end.
K CTRL-TAB
K CHOME SHIFT-END CTRL-INS
K CEND CTRL-INS SHIFT-INS
S MEMOCLIP
# So does a cut with nothing selected: the first line pastes again.  A
# cut of the second line takes it off the text and puts it on the
# clipboard, which pastes it back.
K SHIFT-DEL SHIFT-INS
S MEMOCUT0
K SHIFT-HOME SHIFT-DEL
S MEMOCUT
K SHIFT-INS
S MEMOBACK
K TAB TAB TAB TAB
K ESC
S ASK
K Y
S END
